{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And when a man strikes his slave</b> — Our Rabbis already taught (in the Mekhilta here), by necessity from the reason of <b>for he is his money</b>, that Scripture speaks of a Canaanite slave, and the plain sense of the verse is exactly as they say in truth, for the terms 'slave' and 'maidservant' without qualification never refer to 'his brother the Hebrew' or 'the Hebrewess.' And Scripture says <b>with a rod</b>, with the definite article, because it is the way of one who rules and is a master to have a rod in his hand, and it warns that even though it is a rod of discipline and not an injurious stick, he must be careful with it and not strike even the Canaanite slave with a blow that is not habitual, and this is the meaning of 'under his hand' — that he struck him a great blow so that he died. And there was no need to explain its law, but rather He states that he shall not be exempt on account of the fact that he is his money — <b>he shall surely be avenged</b>, with the same vengeance as for any man who strikes a person and he dies, concerning whom He said (Exodus 21:12) 'he shall surely be put to death.'\n\nAnd by way of the plain sense, <b>but if he stands a day or two days</b> — that the slave will get up and stand on his feet, and therefore it was necessary to say <b>a day or two days</b>, and its meaning is: if on that day or even on the following day he stands on his feet, he shall not be avenged. And its measure is 'only if for a day or for two days,' or 'until a day or until two days,' and there are many like it. Now, at first He said <b>and he dies under his hand</b>, which would imply that he dies at the time of the striking immediately, and He returned to clarify that if he stood on the day of the striking on his feet, or even if he was unable to stand at all on that day but stood on the following day, the master is exempt; but if he did not stand at all, he is liable even though he died on the second day, for this too is 'he died under his hand.' And He did not mention standing on the third day, for once he survived three days the master is exempt, since I can no longer say of him <b>and he dies under his hand</b>.\n\nAnd according to the opinion of our Rabbis (in the Mekhilta here), He mentioned <b>a day or two days</b> to inform us that he must survive twenty-four hours, and its meaning is a full day or two days that are not full days. And had He said only 'a day,' it would have been required that he survive a night and a day like the day of the Torah in the matter of Creation and in Sabbaths and festivals. And 'he stands' (יַעֲמֹד) means 'he remains alive.' Similarly, 'that they may stand many days' (Jeremiah 32:14) means 'they may endure,' and this is the correct and true interpretation."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:13:39.243256211Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}